Z||000006||terryk||07-30-2001||12:26 PM||||In my opinion, GM's stock chips are pretty dang good. Unless the engine is turbocharged where you can increase the boost (the SC engines are limited by the pulley), there's not much to be gained by changing the chip. <P>I believe GM tests 15 cars and sets the calibration to the average. Obviously any one of those 15 engines could be tuned within a inch of it's life and you'd gain more HP, but generally the gain would be minor. Also, GM does run the engines a bit rich overall as protection (cooling). Leaning them out a tad will usually bring some improvements. <P>This doesn't mean you won't see any difference with a chip. If your engine is on the extreme end, the chip might show some improvement or even some loss. There just isn't any "instant-dragster" change that can be made.<P>If you modify the engine, then changes are very useful, but I'm surprised that the companies say, "Oh, you made those changes. Sure, here's a new chip". That's BS. There is no way to make any intelligent changes to the chip without some data from the ECM (O2, INT/BLM, etc.) Sure, they can change the base pulse width to ballpark it for the 90 lb injectors you installed, but without some data, they are just pissing in the wind and taking your money. The worse ones are the changes that are only effective when the coolant is between -40F and 8F. <P>And folks, some of you are running those chips.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
